WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:03.444
Most people can name the President.

2
00:00:03.444 --> 00:00:05.721
Fewer can name their county supervisor.

3
00:00:05.721 --> 00:00:09.050
Almost nobody can name their water district board member.

4
00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:15.648
That's backwards, because the closer a government sits to you, the more it actually touches your life day to day.

5
00:00:15.648 --> 00:00:17.575
Local government isn't one thing.

6
00:00:17.575 --> 00:00:29.313
It's four overlapping layers stacked on top of each other in the same physical space: your city, your county, a scattering of special districts you've probably never heard of, and your school district.

7
00:00:29.313 --> 00:00:35.444
Each one has its own elected officials, its own budget, and its own slice of what happens on your street.

8
00:00:35.444 --> 00:00:37.313
A pothole is the city's problem.

9
00:00:37.313 --> 00:00:38.714
A trial is the county's.

10
00:00:38.714 --> 00:00:43.269
Your tap water might belong to a special district entirely separate from both.

11
00:00:43.269 --> 00:00:48.700
And who's teaching your kids answers to a school board that has nothing to do with the mayor.

12
00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:52.228
Take Torrance as the example running through this series.

13
00:00:52.228 --> 00:01:04.731
It's one of the largest cities in the South Bay, home to roughly a hundred and forty thousand people, and it's unusual for a modern California city in one specific way: it runs almost everything itself.

14
00:01:04.731 --> 00:01:06.340
Its own police department.

15
00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:07.825
Its own fire department.

16
00:01:07.825 --> 00:01:09.187
Its own water utility.

17
00:01:09.187 --> 00:01:11.477
Its own bus system, Torrance Transit.

18
00:01:11.477 --> 00:01:17.605
Most of its South Bay neighbors contract at least some of these out, usually to Los Angeles County.

19
00:01:17.605 --> 00:01:19.090
Torrance mostly doesn't.

20
00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:28.560
That makes it a genuinely useful place to study, because so much of what a city can choose to do or not do is visible right there in its own departments.

21
00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:30.776
Torrance doesn't operate alone, though.

22
00:01:30.776 --> 00:01:45.437
It sits inside Los Angeles County's 4th Supervisorial District, a district so large it covers more than thirty cities and over two million residents, stretching from the Palos Verdes Peninsula through Torrance and down into Long Beach and the Gateway Cities.

23
00:01:45.437 --> 00:01:58.735
It's also one of sixteen member cities in the South Bay Cities Council of Governments, a regional body that coordinates things no single city can solve alone, like transportation funding, water conservation, and homelessness services.

24
00:01:58.735 --> 00:02:09.020
And within its own borders, or close to them, sit school districts, water agencies, and sanitation districts, each with legal authority that the city government simply doesn't have.

25
00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:11.036
Why should any of this matter to you?

26
00:02:11.036 --> 00:02:19.480
Because turnout in local elections is often a small fraction of turnout in presidential ones, which means the votes that do get cast carry outsized weight.

27
00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:32.881
Because the decisions that shape your actual daily experience, whether a road gets repaved, whether a housing project gets approved next door, whether your kid's school gets a new librarian, are almost never made in Washington or even Sacramento.

28
00:02:32.881 --> 00:02:39.800
They're made a few miles from where you're listening to this, often by people who would happily meet with you if you showed up.

29
00:02:39.800 --> 00:03:04.383
Over the next nine episodes, we'll take this apart piece by piece: how city hall actually works, what the county does that the city can't, the invisible special districts that run your water and your buses, who decides what gets built next door, where the money comes from, how school districts fit into all of this, why your local vote matters more than you think, and finally, concretely, how to get involved.

30
00:03:04.383 --> 00:03:07.075
None of it requires becoming a policy expert.

31
00:03:07.075 --> 00:03:09.407
It just requires knowing where to look.

32
00:03:09.407 --> 00:03:18.200
For more, see the City of Torrance's own government pages at torranceca.gov, and the South Bay Cities Council of Governments at southbaycities.org.
